aks came from  the same source scientists later  discovered they were two distinctive  subtypes of ebola zaire and sudan but  whether they were the same or different  they both subsequently vanished yeah  they just vanished totally at least in  humans after the smaller 1979 outbreak  there would be no more documented cases  of ebola in humans for 15 years when it  did return in 1994 it would initially be  just a single scientist infected while  examining a dead chimpanzee in ivory  coast's thai forest sadly though this  pause would turn out to be exactly that  a pause a chance for the virus to gather  its breath and prepare  for the next onslaught because as you  already know ebola did not vanish in  1979 eventually it would come back and  when it did  it'd be deadlier than ever  even during its post-1979 pause ebola  continued to weigh heavily on humanity's  mind if you're old enough you might  remember the scare around infected  monkeys at a lab in reston virginia  where a new strain of ebola was  discovered that fell primates in  eye-watering numbers thankfully the rest  and subtype turned out to cause no  symptoms in the handful of humans it  infected a fact that should go down as  one of history's great near-misses still  the scare kept the virus in the  headlines and nor was it the only ebola  warrior in the uk scientist portend down  managed to accidentally prick himself  with the contaminated needle while he  survived two scientists who managed the  same screw-up in russia weren't so lucky  both went to die horrible and tragic  deaths still as 1994 dawned it was on a  world in which ebola was just an exotic  word one tinged with fear but still  abstract enough to be comfortable  unfortunately for humanity things were  about to get decidedly  uncomfortable at the end of 1994  disturbing reports started filtering  into the city of makoko inkabon of  primates out in the forests dying  horribly not long after that the first  human patients arrived at the hospital  men who'd been hunting for gold near  where the apes had been found and were  now experiencing the tell-tale symptoms  of ebola although its return was a shock  the gabon outbreak was contained rapidly  a mere 52 people fell ill with only 31  dying far fewer than either of the 1976  flare-ups but gabon's brush with death  was only the warm-up act the headline  would be taking place to the south and  east in one of the viruses original  homes zaire the 1995 outbreak in the  town of kikwit was the resurgence that  virologists had feared after a charcoal  maker came back from the forest feeling  ill ebola exploded across the region  multiple hospitals became clearing  houses for the disease in kikwit and  surrounding villages swats of people  fell ill desperate to stop the spread  the government blocked the road to  kinshasa by the time the epidemic was  brought under control panic had swept  the world and 254 people were dead and  this time there would be no vast gap  between outbreaks the rest of the 1990s  and into the 2000s saw a kaleidoscope of  ebola flare up some big some small until  barely a year could go by without a  bunch of villagers dying horribly as the  outbreaks increased they also spread to  new countries like uganda and the  republic of the congo and the number of  infected also started creeping up the  2000 to 2001 uganda outbreak saw the  number of patients in a single event  climb over 400 for the first time  meanwhile yet another variant emerged  this time around the town of bundy bugio  taking the total number of human  affecting ebola viruses up to four by  the end of the first decade of the 21st  century over a thousand people were  known to have died of ebola a low number  compared to say gobert 19 but high  enough to represent a real worry still  this worry did come with a silver lining  even as new records were broken for  infections in a single outbreak the  numbers themselves remain manageable bad  as it was for those of the epicenter  even 425 cases in one go wasn't  apocalyptically bad and while ebola  refused to go away there was some hope  that we could learn to live with it  after all we thought we understood it  that it was a disease of the forests and  villages that it was scary as heck but  could easily be stopped before it got  out of hand  how wrong we were just like all those  magazines in 2016 preparing madam  president front covers humanity in this  era had badly misjudged things come 2014  ebola would go from freaky thing it's  fun to read about on cracked.com to the  bringer of west africa's local  apocalypse  [Music]  by any arctic the death of a mill omono  was a gut-wrenching tragedy just two  years old emil passed away on december  28 2013 in guinea a country of around 13  million people on the west african coast  many many kilometers away from congo's  tropical forests sad as this lamil's  death was though it was just a preview  of what would soon become one of the  biggest tragedies in the region's  history the boy you see hadn't just died  young he'd died of ebola the first time  the disease claimed a life in west  africa and it certainly wouldn't be the  last within weeks of emil's death half  of his family had also died killed by a  virus as swift as it was brutal after  that came the mourners then the  healthcare workers before long the death  toll in guinea was creeping up it would  take the authorities until march 2014 to  recognize the deaths for what they were  part of a new ebola epidemic one  striking a country that had never  experienced it before yet it wouldn't  stop at just guinea by the end of the  month the virus was cropping up in the  country's crowded capital the first time  ebola had ever attacked an urban setting  but it would be another funeral that  made the disease start spreading like  blood red wildfire given guinea's  fragile health system many of the  viruses victims turned instead to faith  healers the most famous of whom was  sierra leone's mendenhall when she too  died of ebola the funeral was massive  people came from all over guinea sierra  leone and liberia to pay their respects  respect that as tradition dictated  involved contact with her body sadly  that was just what the virus needed  mendenhall's funeral was a super  spreader event sending dozens back to  their home countries with ebola already  working its dark magic in their  bloodstreams by may both sierra leone  and liberia announced they were fighting  outbreaks with things in sierra leone  being particularly acute the hospital in  the country's second city kanema was  soon overrun and with hygiene practices  not being followed ebola soon began to  spread among patients workers and  visitors alike as west africa's death  toll swept into the thousands cases  began appearing in nigeria and senegal  followed a few months later by mali by  the end of 2014 9 000 west africans have  been killed by the rampaging virus  thousands and thousands more than during  even the deadliest previous outbreak and  as the death toll skyrocketed so too did  the conspiracy theories yep apparently  pre-covert humanity was just as awful as  its pandemic counterparts and not  believing stupid bull from social media  in guinea for instance a conspiracy  theory that ebola was a smoke screen for  white people to kill west africans led  to eight aid workers being murdered in  the cities meanwhile the rumor that  patients were taken into hospitals to  have their organs harvested resulted in  people escaping isolation wards thus  further spreading the disease it was a  tidal wave of disinformation a tsunami  of horse droppings that only caused the  epidemic to get ever worse yet it wasn't  just confined to west africa even as the  disease consumed entire towns in guinea  an equally deranged form of panic was  taking root thousands of kilometers away  yep it's time we turned our attention to  the global capital of social media  freakouts  america  in september 2014 thomas eric duncan  stepped off a plane in texas unaware of  the blind terror he'd soon be trailing  in his wake duncan you see was a  liberian national and he hadn't stepped  off the plane alone oh no inside him was  a stowaway a member of the file of iris  family picked up in his home country and  when he finally began showing symptoms  he'd kick off a health scare unseen in  america since the 2001 anthrax attacks  the media freak out following the  hospitalization and subsequent gruesome  death of duncan was  off the charts shortly before british  nurse william booley had likewise  unwittingly brought the virus into  britain well around the same time  another aid worker tested positive in  spain for a brief period the news was  full of maps and charts showing ominous  red dots in places like texas new york  and britain places where new cases had  been confirmed millions convinced  themselves that it would soon be  sweeping across america yet thanks to  better funded health systems and  different burial traditions ebola never  took root there there was no wave in  europe beyond the infection of two  nurses carrying the duncan there was no  community transmission in the u.s but  that was cold comfort for west africa as  2015 began the virus continued to chart  its destructive path eventually 28 000  cases would be confirmed yet even as  this catastrophe unfolded another more  hopeful tale was quietly being written  the story of how we at last began to  master this awful illness the search for  an ebola vaccine began way back in  mid-1980s fittingly in marburg germany  where one of ebola's cousins had first  been detected in humans there heinz  feldman was working on filoviruses when  he learned of an astounding breakthrough  over in yale a scientist named john jack  rose had figured out how to use a kind  of livestock virus known as vsv as a  delivery system for vaccines intrigued  feldman began dabbling with vsb himself  seeing if he couldn't get it to carry  the ebola glycoprotein at this stage the  work was mostly theoretical yet it  clearly stayed with feldman in the early  2000s he ran a test whereby he used vsv  to infect a bunch of mice with ebola's  glycoprotein before exposing them and a  control group to the virus itself unlike  their unfortunate counterparts the vsb  infected rodents all survived and so  began the long and torturous path  towards an ebola vaccine there was the  2005 study by tom geissberg which  repeated the mice experiments in  primates and again revealed that vsv  deliveries seemed to offer protection  and there was the incident in 2009 when  a german researcher accidentally pricked  himself with ebola affected needle and  was quickly given the experimental  vaccine as a precaution her survival  showed that it was safe to use in humans  but the biggest boost came with the 2014  outbreak prior to the tragedy in west  africa no government or pharma company  had wanted to finance an ebola vaccine  the disease  just cropped up too rarely and infected  too few people to make it profitable but  as the world watched the carnage  unfolding in guinea sierra leone and  liberia that calculation changed at the  end of 2014 the company merck finally  agreed to run a trial and that was how  teams from medicine science francieres  came to be on the ground in guinea in  early 2015 offering the experimental  vaccine to anyone who needed it to be  clear by this time the epidemic was  already winding down improved sanitation  and a widespread understanding that  traditional burials were contributing to  the viruses spread had slowed its pace  there's no suggesting the vaccine ended  the outbreak what it did do though was  provide solid proof in a real world  setting that the damn thing really  worked in july 2015 respected journal  the lancet published the study's  findings taking humankind one giant step  closer to a world without ebola but  while this was truly an incredible  breakthrough it came too late for many  in west africa as the epidemic receded  it left behind the sort of death toll  usually reserved for a war over 11 000  people had died while many of the 17 000  survivors were left suffering a myriad  of complications which today we might  call long ebola in short it had been a  catastrophe a humanitarian disaster on a  grand and depressing scale it was also  thankfully perhaps the last major ebola  epidemic the world will ever face with  the vaccine now proven to be both safe  and effective the path has been laid for  a rapid response to further outbreaks  when the virus reared its head again  just four years later in drc teams were  on the ground armed with shots within  just eight days over a quarter of a  million people were vaccinated and while  over 2000 people still died the virus  stalled out with far fewer fatalities  than in 2014. nor was the vaccine the  only leap forward in 2019 a similar  real-world study was conducted with  experimental treatments using monoclonal  antibodies during an outbreak of zaire  ebola virus astonishingly researchers  showed they raised survival rates to a  staggering 90 percent by the end of 2020  the fda had finally approved two ebola  treatments and one vaccine with a  separate vaccine targeting the sudan sub  type already under development in  england and that's where things  regarding ebola stand today on the  threshold of perhaps a grey new era one  in which this disease of nightmares will  soon be reduced to a treatable if  unpleasant illness it's a change that  can't come soon enough since it first  reared its ugly head in 1976 ebola has  been responsible for tens of thousands  of deaths nearly all of them on the  african continent in this it has been  helped by autocratic leaders and corrupt  governments the kind that pour so little  money into health care that overcrowded  hospitals become places that spread the  disease rather than contain it and  honestly this might be a bowler's real  tragedy since the late 1970s we've known  that the best way to stop ebola is  simple using gloves isolating patients  and giving healthcare workers proper  protective equipment yet thanks to  poverty and neglect it's been able to  crop up time and time again to kill  those already living on the margins the  fight humanity has waged against ebola  might be one we're on the verge of  winning but until we tackle the root  causes the factors that drove it to  become so deadly in 2014 will sadly  remain in place ready and waiting for  the next nightmare virus to arise  so i hope you enjoyed today's video  before you leave let me tell you about a  new channel that i'm doing called into  the shadows so maybe the world isn't  dark enough for you well good news it  absolutely is and if you'd like to know  more about the horrible things that  humans have been doing to each other  since well time immemorial well please  check out that new channel into the  shadows from land mines to penal  colonies to horrific diseases if it's  horrific we cover it check it out  through the link in the description  below again it's called into the shadows  and thank you for watching
 

